```js
form.addEventListener("submit", (e) => {
  e.preventDefault();

  const name = nameInput.value.trim();
  const assignment = assignmentInput.value.trim();

  const row = document.createElement("tr");
  row.innerHTML = `<td>${name}</td><td>${assignment}</td>`;
  submissionsTableBody.appendChild(row);

  form.reset();
});
```

If you’re sending the data to a server with `fetch`, append the row inside the successful `.then()`/`await` block; share your current submit handler and table HTML if you want a precise fix.

Create the row only after `fetch` succeeds, ideally after checking `res.ok`, and use the returned submission data if the API sends it back:

```js
form.addEventListener("submit", async (e) => {
  e.preventDefault();

  const res = await fetch("/api/submissions", {
    method: "POST",
    body: new FormData(form)
  });

  if (!res.ok) {
    throw new Error("Could not save submission");
  }

  const submission = await res.json(); // e.g. { studentName, assignment }

  const row = document.createElement("tr");
  row.innerHTML = `
    <td>${submission.studentName}</td>
    <td>${submission.assignment}</td>
  `;
  submissionsTableBody.appendChild(row);

  form.reset();
});
```

If your endpoint doesn’t return JSON, read the values from the form before resetting and use those to build the row.

Yes—capture the values before `fetch`, then append the row only after `res.ok` confirms the save succeeded:

```js
form.addEventListener("submit", async (e) => {
  e.preventDefault();

  const student = studentInput.value.trim();
  const assignment = assignmentInput.value.trim();

  try {
    const res = await fetch("/api/submissions", {
      method: "POST",
      body: new FormData(form)
    });

    if (!res.ok) throw new Error(`Save failed: ${res.status}`);

    const row = document.createElement("tr");
    row.innerHTML = `<td>${student}</td><td>${assignment}</td>`;
    submissionsTableBody.appendChild(row);
    form.reset();
  } catch (error) {
    console.error(error);
    alert("The submission could not be saved.");
  }
});
```

Using `textContent` instead of `innerHTML` for each cell is safer if those values may contain user-entered HTML.

Yes—disable the button during the request and re-enable it in `finally`, so it also recovers after an error:

```js
form.addEventListener("submit", async (e) => {
  e.preventDefault();

  const student = studentInput.value.trim();
  const assignment = assignmentInput.value.trim();

  submitButton.disabled = true;

  try {
    const res = await fetch("/api/submissions", {
      method: "POST",
      body: new FormData(form)
    });

    if (!res.ok) throw new Error(`Save failed: ${res.status}`);

    const row = document.createElement("tr");
    const studentCell = document.createElement("td");
    const assignmentCell = document.createElement("td");

    studentCell.textContent = student;
    assignmentCell.textContent = assignment;
    row.append(studentCell, assignmentCell);
    submissionsTableBody.appendChild(row);

    form.reset();
  } catch (error) {
    console.error(error);
    alert("The submission could not be saved.");
  } finally {
    submitButton.disabled = false;
  }
});
```

This prevents rapid duplicate submissions from the same form; the server should still enforce uniqueness if duplicates must be rejected reliably.

Yes—handle everything in the `submit` listener, since pressing Enter can submit the form without a button click. Set a status element to “Saving…”, disable the button immediately, and clear or update that message in `finally`:

```js
const status = document.querySelector("#save-status");

form.addEventListener("submit", async (e) => {
  e.preventDefault();
  if (submitButton.disabled) return; // guards rapid duplicate submits

  const student = studentInput.value.trim();
  const assignment = assignmentInput.value.trim();

  submitButton.disabled = true;
  status.textContent = "Saving…";

  try {
    const res = await fetch("/api/submissions", {
      method: "POST",
      body: new FormData(form)
    });
    if (!res.ok) throw new Error(`Save failed: ${res.status}`);

    const row = document.createElement("tr");
    for (const value of [student, assignment]) {
      const cell = document.createElement("td");
      cell.textContent = value;
      row.appendChild(cell);
    }
    submissionsTableBody.appendChild(row);
    form.reset();
    status.textContent = "Saved.";
  } catch (err) {
    console.error(err);
    status.textContent = "Could not save the submission.";
  } finally {
    submitButton.disabled = false;
  }
});
```

Add `<span id="save-status" aria-live="polite"></span>` near the form so screen readers receive status updates.

Generate the key once for the logical submission and reuse it for any retry:

```js
const idempotencyKey = crypto.randomUUID();

const res = await fetch("/api/submissions", {
  method: "POST",
  headers: { "Idempotency-Key": idempotencyKey },
  body: new FormData(form)
});
```

A minimal Express example can cache completed requests by key (use a database/Redis with an expiry in production):

```js
const completed = new Map();

app.post("/api/submissions", express.urlencoded({ extended: true }), (req, res) => {
  const key = req.get("Idempotency-Key");
  if (!key) return res.status(400).json({ error: "Missing Idempotency-Key" });

  const previous = completed.get(key);
  if (previous) return res.status(previous.status).json(previous.body);

  // Save to your database here; enforce a unique (student, assignment) constraint too.
  const result = { student: req.body.student, assignment: req.body.assignment };
  const response = { status: 201, body: result };
  completed.set(key, response);

  res.status(response.status).json(response.body);
});
```

If the first response is lost after the database save, retrying with the same key returns the cached result instead of inserting another submission.

Yes—make the idempotency row and submission insert part of one transaction, with the idempotency row locked before deciding what to do. The unique constraint causes a concurrent request with the same key to wait until the first transaction commits, after which it reads the completed response:

```sql
CREATE EXTENSION IF NOT EXISTS pgcrypto;

CREATE TABLE idempotency_keys (
  key         TEXT PRIMARY KEY,
  request_hash TEXT NOT NULL,
  state       TEXT NOT NULL CHECK (state IN ('pending', 'completed')),
  status_code INTEGER,
  response    JSONB
);

CREATE TABLE submissions (
  id         BIGSERIAL PRIMARY KEY,
  student    TEXT NOT NULL,
  assignment TEXT NOT NULL,
  created_at TIMESTAMPTZ NOT NULL DEFAULT now(),
  UNIQUE (student, assignment)
);
```

Example using Node’s `pg` client:

```js
async function createSubmission(req, res) {
  const client = await pool.connect();

  const { student, assignment } = req.body;

  // Compute this on the server from a canonical representation.
  const requestHash = crypto
    .createHash("sha256")
    .update(JSON.stringify({ student, assignment }))
    .digest("hex");

  const key = req.get("Idempotency-Key");
  if (!key) {
    client.release();
    return res.status(400).json({ error: "Missing Idempotency-Key" });
  }

  try {
    await client.query("BEGIN");

    // Only one transaction can insert a given key.
    // A concurrent INSERT waits for the first transaction to finish.
    await client.query(
      `INSERT INTO idempotency_keys (key, request_hash, state)
       VALUES ($1, $2, 'pending')
       ON CONFLICT (key) DO NOTHING`,
      [key, requestHash]
    );

    // FOR UPDATE serializes all requests using this key.
    const { rows } = await client.query(
      `SELECT key, request_hash, state, status_code, response
       FROM idempotency_keys
       WHERE key = $1
       FOR UPDATE`,
      [key]
    );

    const record = rows[0];

    if (record.request_hash !== requestHash) {
      await client.query("ROLLBACK");
      return res.status(409).json({
        error: "Idempotency key was reused with different request data"
      });
    }

    // This is a retry after the original transaction completed.
    if (record.state === "completed") {
      await client.query("COMMIT");
      return res.status(record.status_code).json(record.response);
    }

    const inserted = await client.query(
      `INSERT INTO submissions (student, assignment)
       VALUES ($1, $2)
       ON CONFLICT (student, assignment) DO NOTHING
       RETURNING id, student, assignment, created_at`,
      [student, assignment]
    );

    let statusCode;
    let responseBody;

    if (inserted.rowCount === 1) {
      statusCode = 201;
      responseBody = inserted.rows[0];
    } else {
      statusCode = 409;
      responseBody = {
        error: "This student already has a submission for this assignment"
      };
    }

    // Store the exact response so retries return the same result.
    await client.query(
      `UPDATE idempotency_keys
       SET state = 'completed',
           status_code = $2,
           response = $3::jsonb
       WHERE key = $1`,
      [key, statusCode, JSON.stringify(responseBody)]
    );

    await client.query("COMMIT");
    return res.status(statusCode).json(responseBody);
  } catch (error) {
    await client.query("ROLLBACK");
    console.error(error);
    return res.status(500).json({ error: "Could not save submission" });
  } finally {
    client.release();
  }
}
```

If two identical requests arrive simultaneously, one creates the key and submission while the other waits on the unique key, then returns the first transaction’s stored response; if the first transaction rolls back, the second can safely perform the insert instead.

Keep the `(student, assignment)` unique constraint and, after claiming the idempotency key, use `ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING`; a conflict means no new row was inserted, so fetch and return the existing submission with `409`:

```js
const inserted = await client.query(`
  INSERT INTO submissions (student, assignment)
  VALUES ($1, $2)
  ON CONFLICT (student, assignment) DO NOTHING
  RETURNING id, student, assignment, created_at
`, [student, assignment]);

let statusCode, responseBody;

if (inserted.rowCount === 1) {
  statusCode = 201;
  responseBody = inserted.rows[0]; // genuinely created
} else {
  const existing = await client.query(`
    SELECT id, student, assignment, created_at
    FROM submissions
    WHERE student = $1 AND assignment = $2
  `, [student, assignment]);

  statusCode = 409;
  responseBody = {
    error: "Submission already exists",
    submission: existing.rows[0]
  };
}

// Still cache this exact 409 response under the new idempotency key.
await client.query(`
  UPDATE idempotency_keys
  SET state = 'completed', status_code = $2, response = $3::jsonb
  WHERE key = $1
`, [key, statusCode, JSON.stringify(responseBody)]);
```

Commit the transaction and send `res.status(statusCode).json(responseBody)`; a retry using that new key then returns the same cached `409`, while a retry using the original key returns the original `201` response.

Handle `409` as an expected application response, not as a network failure, and read the returned `submission`:

```js
const status = document.querySelector("#submission-status");

async function submitAssignment(data) {
  status.textContent = "Submitting…";

  try {
    const response = await fetch("/submissions", {
      method: "POST",
      headers: {
        "Content-Type": "application/json",
        "Idempotency-Key": crypto.randomUUID()
      },
      body: JSON.stringify(data)
    });

    const body = await response.json();

    if (response.ok) {
      status.textContent = "Submission created successfully.";
      showSubmission(body);
    } else if (response.status === 409 && body.error === "already_exists") {
      status.textContent = "This assignment was already submitted.";
      showSubmission(body.submission);
    } else {
      status.textContent = body.error || "Submission could not be saved.";
    }
  } catch {
    status.textContent = "Network error. Please try again using the same submission key.";
  }
}
```

Use an element such as `<div id="submission-status" role="status" aria-live="polite"></div>`; keep the original idempotency key for retries after a timeout, but a deliberate new submission attempt should receive a new key.

